Thank you very much. Um, I'm delighted to be with all of you.

00:02:00.770 --> 00:02:03.200
One of the things that we know is

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.580
that ethical made- decision-making is all around us, isn't?

00:02:07.580 --> 00:02:10.580
We see it everyday and what we try to do in the,

00:02:10.580 --> 00:02:12.770
in the half-hour of this webinar is give

00:02:12.770 --> 00:02:16.340
some examples of some analysis of these situations,

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:18.425
and what we might do about it.

00:02:18.425 --> 00:02:21.845
This is one that affected me personally.

00:02:21.845 --> 00:02:24.260
Um, second hand cigarette smoke.

00:02:24.260 --> 00:02:26.285
This was several years ago.

00:02:26.285 --> 00:02:31.970
And I was giving a decision analysis course to industry helping people think about um,

00:02:31.970 --> 00:02:35.430
um, it's, it's, it's advancing on the timer.

00:02:35.430 --> 00:02:38.320
Thinking about this and during one of the breaks,

00:02:38.320 --> 00:02:41.980
as senior executive from one of the tobacco companies took me aside and said,

00:02:41.980 --> 00:02:45.290
"You have a Stanford PhD? "Yes I do."

00:02:45.290 --> 00:02:47.455
And here comes the, uh,

00:02:47.455 --> 00:02:49.385
request from this person.

00:02:49.385 --> 00:02:52.970
We need someone with your credentials to testify in our behalf.

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:58.475
Uh, you certainly believe that secondhand cigarette smoke is not harmful, don't you?

00:02:58.475 --> 00:03:00.470
It's very diverse. It's very,

00:03:00.470 --> 00:03:02.540
it diluted and transient.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:06.995
We have a lot of money we can pay to you for testimony as like this.

00:03:06.995 --> 00:03:08.585
Will you please do it for us.

00:03:08.585 --> 00:03:10.510
We'll make it well worth your while.

00:03:10.510 --> 00:03:15.680
Decision analysis with some low probabilities will be a really convincing and

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:18.380
convince people that second hand cigarette smoke

00:03:18.380 --> 00:03:21.765
is not a liability we should be held accountable for.

00:03:21.765 --> 00:03:23.760
It's not about thoughts,

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:25.860
mindsets, abstractions, or philosophies.

00:03:25.860 --> 00:03:29.115
Ethics is about actions and consequences.

00:03:29.115 --> 00:03:32.025
Without decisions, what does ethics even mean?

00:03:32.025 --> 00:03:35.635
Number two, ethics is about people not about things.

00:03:35.635 --> 00:03:38.670
Do ciga- are- do cigarettes make decisions?

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:41.615
Do cigarettes have any ethical content whatsoever?

00:03:41.615 --> 00:03:44.510
We think not. We think that things are not what are

00:03:44.510 --> 00:03:48.680
engaging in actions or behaviors, uh, it's people.

00:03:48.680 --> 00:03:52.340
The third thing is ethics is ultimately personal,

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:57.320
related to personal decision making or personal contribution to group decision making.

00:03:57.320 --> 00:04:01.870
Again, without decisions, what is ethics?

00:04:01.870 --> 00:04:07.500
Okay. Ethics needs to be driven therefore by a well crafted ethical code we think.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.620
In the course that we teach,

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:12.680
everyone writes a personal ethical code of action based on

00:04:12.680 --> 00:04:16.510
the solid ethical principles and theories that we put forward.

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:20.300
And that code is a key deliverable in the course,

00:04:20.300 --> 00:04:21.350
and it's proven to be one of

00:04:21.350 --> 00:04:25.635
the most significant penetrating emotional things people can do,

00:04:25.635 --> 00:04:27.845
people have told us that years later.

00:04:27.845 --> 00:04:32.610
Many people carry and modify their ethical codes for many years into the future,

00:04:32.610 --> 00:04:37.520
and that's an interesting comment that we get back from students years into the future.

00:04:37.520 --> 00:04:39.670
As we think about ethics,

00:04:39.670 --> 00:04:43.415
we like to think about the decisions related to, ah,

00:04:43.415 --> 00:04:48.545
every day life and business life as falling into three basic,

00:04:48.545 --> 00:04:52.505
ah, ah, categories or having through three aspects.

00:04:52.505 --> 00:04:55.190
One is prudential, and the prudential aspect of

00:04:55.190 --> 00:04:58.235
decision making is what we teach in decision analysis.

00:04:58.235 --> 00:05:00.215
What's the best thing I can be doing?

00:05:00.215 --> 00:05:02.105
What benefits me the most?

00:05:02.105 --> 00:05:08.385
Prudence. Making good decisions and maximizing the probability of good outcomes.

00:05:08.385 --> 00:05:10.470
What do I gain and what do I lose?

00:05:10.470 --> 00:05:15.365
So, we like to look at these ethical decisions such as the testimony on cigarette smoke,

00:05:15.365 --> 00:05:18.060
from a potential point of view.

00:05:18.850 --> 00:05:23.305
One of the things when we do that we, we,

00:05:23.305 --> 00:05:27.349
we come up with a notion that prudential decisions,

00:05:27.349 --> 00:05:31.420
um, are those that involve regret.

00:05:31.420 --> 00:05:33.550
Jeez, I wish I'd had done that,

00:05:33.550 --> 00:05:35.405
I could have made a lot more money,

00:05:35.405 --> 00:05:37.085
those kinds of things.

00:05:37.085 --> 00:05:42.055
So, when you're thinking about regret your thinking about prudence,

00:05:42.055 --> 00:05:46.020
and that's the first olympic ring of ethics would call it.

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:50.240
The second one over on the left we wanna think about legal aspects,

00:05:50.240 --> 00:05:52.505
and legal aspects have to do with,

00:05:52.505 --> 00:05:55.480
are there any laws for or against this.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.720
We give a lot of attention to the notion that the legal ball,

00:05:59.720 --> 00:06:03.545
or the legal ring is quite distinct from the ethical ring, isn't?

00:06:03.545 --> 00:06:05.030
The way we like to,

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.705
uh, the way we like to, uh,

00:06:06.705 --> 00:06:10.640
emphasize this is, do you want these types of people,

00:06:10.640 --> 00:06:14.270
the people who were responsible for laws or mandates writing

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:18.170
your ethical code for you and most students and most people say,

00:06:18.170 --> 00:06:19.460
" No, I don't want that.

00:06:19.460 --> 00:06:21.550
I don't want a political process,

00:06:21.550 --> 00:06:24.395
a lawmaking process writing my ethical code.

00:06:24.395 --> 00:06:27.530
I wanna write my own ethical code and I wanna think about

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:32.110
the law at the same time but distinct from ethics."

00:06:32.110 --> 00:06:37.130
Okay? When you think about it that way one of the things we talk a lot about,

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:41.539
and we talked about in another course that we've offered here at Stanford is, okay,

00:06:41.539 --> 00:06:46.565
would part of your ethical code you'd be willing to impose on others by force?

00:06:46.565 --> 00:06:49.370
If your ethical code doesn't, uh,

00:06:49.370 --> 00:06:52.190
doesn't include something like child sexual abuse,

00:06:52.190 --> 00:06:55.930
would you be willing to impose that on others by force? I would.

00:06:55.930 --> 00:06:59.810
If your ethical co- code says I don't imbibe

00:06:59.810 --> 00:07:03.745
alcohol would you be willing to impose that others by force? I wouldn't.

00:07:03.745 --> 00:07:06.740
So, we like to think a lot about legal as

00:07:06.740 --> 00:07:10.130
part of an ethical code that's imposed on others by force.

00:07:10.130 --> 00:07:14.990
That's not what we really wanna talk about completely when we talk about ethics

00:07:14.990 --> 00:07:19.980
once we've fought through the prudential and legal aspects of these decisions.

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:21.900
The last one is ethics.

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:25.190
And ethics boils down to right and wrong,

00:07:25.190 --> 00:07:29.810
acceptable and unacceptable, and when we're thinking about ethical decisions,

00:07:29.810 --> 00:07:31.685
we're thinking about remorse.

00:07:31.685 --> 00:07:34.460
We're thinking about lying awake nights

00:07:34.460 --> 00:07:38.540
wishing perhaps that we had not done that or we had done that.

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:41.480
So, remorse is associated with ethics,

00:07:41.480 --> 00:07:44.165
regret is associated with prudence.

00:07:44.165 --> 00:07:49.730
So, let's go back and think about how I thought about the, uh, the,

00:07:49.730 --> 00:07:55.850
uh, testifying, um, in behalf of the- of tobacco companies on second hand smoke.

00:07:55.850 --> 00:07:59.425
Prudentiality, was that prudence, prudential?

00:07:59.425 --> 00:08:03.005
Yeah. I could've made a heck of a lot of money doing that.

00:08:03.005 --> 00:08:05.450
I could have gotten a lot of notoriety and so forth.

00:08:05.450 --> 00:08:10.100
So, from our prudential aspect had I taken that assignment and done that,

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:14.325
it would've been very much in my personal economic interests.

00:08:14.325 --> 00:08:18.000
Legal, any legal implications to that decision?

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.480
Probably not, probably not unless there was some perjury involved.

00:08:23.480 --> 00:08:28.200
Okay? Ethical, right and wrong.

00:08:28.720 --> 00:08:30.860
Would it have been ethical,

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:33.980
would it have been right for me to lend

00:08:33.980 --> 00:08:37.415
my support to that kind of a thing? Would you have done it?

00:08:37.415 --> 00:08:42.275
I didn't do it,

00:08:42.275 --> 00:08:43.750
I turned it down.

00:08:43.750 --> 00:08:46.510
When I look back on it is there regret?

00:08:46.510 --> 00:08:51.940
Well, there's regret when I think about the pecuniary rewards that I might have gotten,

00:08:51.940 --> 00:08:53.290
but there's no remorse,

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:55.245
I know I did the right thing.

00:08:55.245 --> 00:09:00.140
And these kinds of ethical decisions are with us every day, aren't they?

00:09:00.690 --> 00:09:03.210
A little bit of background on me.

00:09:03.210 --> 00:09:08.110
My father was the general manager of a large open-pit copper mine in the state of Nevada.

00:09:08.110 --> 00:09:09.595
A place called Weed Heights, Nevada.

00:09:09.595 --> 00:09:16.110
You'll note the arrow on the diagram that will become significant in a few minutes, okay?

00:09:16.110 --> 00:09:21.040
That large open-pit copper mine has been closed for roughly three decades.

00:09:21.040 --> 00:09:23.080
It's now a superfund site.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.205
It pollutes groundwater.

00:09:25.205 --> 00:09:29.530
Ah, the gravel and other stuff blows around in the wind and,

00:09:29.530 --> 00:09:33.280
and, and it gets around that Valley.

00:09:33.280 --> 00:09:35.960
Kind of an interesting question is,

00:09:35.960 --> 00:09:41.345
did my dad and his colleagues do anything unethical engaging in the mining business?

00:09:41.345 --> 00:09:43.200
Well, was it prudential?

00:09:43.200 --> 00:09:44.745
Yes. Was it legal?

00:09:44.745 --> 00:09:47.910
Yes. Was it the right thing to do?

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.185
Did they know that they were going to, ah, pollute?

00:09:51.185 --> 00:09:53.375
Did they know that they were going to,

00:09:53.375 --> 00:09:55.805
ah, to harm the environment? They did not know that.

00:09:55.805 --> 00:10:01.210
The state of information that they had in those days was very low on groundwater,

00:10:01.210 --> 00:10:05.490
on, uh, uh, migration of surficial water and so forth.

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:10.700
I would argue there was nothing unethical given the state of information that they had.

00:10:10.700 --> 00:10:13.655
Would it be unethical to behave that way today?

00:10:13.655 --> 00:10:16.620
It might well be, because we know more.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:20.105
So, ethics is, is rational as well.

00:10:20.105 --> 00:10:24.695
I do know, and this is kind of a funny story using a Will Rogers quote,

00:10:24.695 --> 00:10:27.685
"When I moved from, ah, Nevada to California,

00:10:27.685 --> 00:10:32.220
here at Stanford I lowered the average IQ of both places."

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:36.440
When I finished at Stanford the first job that I got,

00:10:36.440 --> 00:10:38.450
and this is in the public domain so I'm not,

00:10:38.450 --> 00:10:40.625
div- divulging any confidences,

00:10:40.625 --> 00:10:43.490
was at the Environmental Protection Agency,

00:10:43.490 --> 00:10:45.170
and there was a,

00:10:45.170 --> 00:10:47.050
a pesticide named DDT,

00:10:47.050 --> 00:10:49.255
many of you have known about that,

00:10:49.255 --> 00:10:52.415
which was canceled a couple years prior to this.

00:10:52.415 --> 00:10:55.670
The next one in line was a pesticide called

00:10:55.670 --> 00:11:00.320
Chlordane which is very effective against termites and corn worms and so forth.

00:11:00.320 --> 00:11:03.170
The motivation for canceling it was it may be

00:11:03.170 --> 00:11:07.075
carcinogenic and it may have long-term harm on the environment.

00:11:07.075 --> 00:11:10.180
So, would you have taken this jump?

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:13.330
I was not ethically sensitive in those days.

00:11:13.330 --> 00:11:14.395
It was my first job.

00:11:14.395 --> 00:11:18.865
I did take it and I have no expertise in the,

00:11:18.865 --> 00:11:22.805
in the in human biology or the environmental biology.

00:11:22.805 --> 00:11:26.380
My job was to figure out how much economic,

00:11:26.380 --> 00:11:30.400
um, dislocation would happen if the pesticide was canceled.

00:11:30.400 --> 00:11:34.550
So, I don't think I did anything unethical.

00:11:35.450 --> 00:11:38.490
Now, the next point uh,

00:11:38.490 --> 00:11:42.450
to be made over and over again and, and in the uh,

00:11:42.450 --> 00:11:46.605
in our everyday ethics in our course we really worry about this,

00:11:46.605 --> 00:11:50.190
and that is ethics ex-extends well beyond business doesn't it.

00:11:50.190 --> 00:11:52.890
We very much consider that.

00:11:52.890 --> 00:11:56.085
Let's think of another eth-ethical question.

00:11:56.085 --> 00:11:59.910
Would you cheat on your spouse or your significant other?

00:11:59.910 --> 00:12:05.500
Would you do that? Would you hook up at a bar or at work?

00:12:05.720 --> 00:12:08.850
What if you were committed to a significant other,

00:12:08.850 --> 00:12:10.125
would that make a difference?

00:12:10.125 --> 00:12:13.270
What if you were a free electron uncommitted?

00:12:13.640 --> 00:12:17.085
The ethical codes that are students right here,

00:12:17.085 --> 00:12:19.425
they focus on this. Some would some would.

00:12:19.425 --> 00:12:22.890
Ethics is personal, it has to deal with decisions.

00:12:22.890 --> 00:12:26.010
You remember the arrow in the mind.

00:12:26.010 --> 00:12:29.940
I grew up in the state of Nevada where prostitution was legal.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:35.145
[NOISE] And the question that comes it's an ethical question,

00:12:35.145 --> 00:12:39.540
is would you go to a brothel or equivalent? Would you do that?

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:43.095
The county that I grew up in while in county Nevada,

00:12:43.095 --> 00:12:45.555
still has prostitution legal.

00:12:45.555 --> 00:12:48.375
And if you look at that arrow about seven miles up there,

00:12:48.375 --> 00:12:50.085
there was a legal brothel.

00:12:50.085 --> 00:12:52.950
The famous Mustang Ranch was pretty close,

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:56.010
it was maybe 40 miles up that road.

00:12:56.010 --> 00:12:59.250
One of the things we noticed is those places always

00:12:59.250 --> 00:13:01.500
had cars in the parking lot and my dad used

00:13:01.500 --> 00:13:06.120
to complain they're my employees' cars. Would you do that?

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:09.190
It's a question for your ethical code, isn't it?

00:13:10.610 --> 00:13:13.020
And when we think about that,

00:13:13.020 --> 00:13:17.070
we offer three other distinctions that we need to think about in

00:13:17.070 --> 00:13:21.495
considering and pondering and analyzing the ethics of the decisions we might make.

00:13:21.495 --> 00:13:24.645
The first dimension is lying, deception.

00:13:24.645 --> 00:13:27.780
So, when we think about going to a brothel or we think

00:13:27.780 --> 00:13:32.190
about just spoiling the environment, are we lying?

00:13:32.190 --> 00:13:34.125
Are we lying to others?

00:13:34.125 --> 00:13:36.825
Are we lying to ourselves?

00:13:36.825 --> 00:13:42.630
To lying- understanding deception lying is a big part of understanding ethics.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:46.740
Stealing, violating property, is the second aspect.

00:13:46.740 --> 00:13:52.140
Are we doing something to violate the property of others? Are we taking it?

00:13:52.140 --> 00:13:54.270
And the third aspect of ethics,

00:13:54.270 --> 00:13:57.870
we know um, um, this one, harming.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:00.270
Violating the person of someone else.

00:14:00.270 --> 00:14:03.930
Most ethical decisions and most dimensions of

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:10.015
those can be categorized in lying and deception, stealing and harming.

00:14:10.015 --> 00:14:14.030
And we give it a good deal of attention on our analysis to these things.

00:14:14.030 --> 00:14:16.850
Kinda free on each of these dimensions.

00:14:16.850 --> 00:14:20.800
There's a good chance that you're making an ethically respectable decision.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:24.750
Okay. This raises another point,

00:14:24.750 --> 00:14:29.700
and that is that morals are quite different from ethics. What are morals?

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:31.875
What's the definition of moray?

00:14:31.875 --> 00:14:35.100
It's what's acceptable in your society.

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.530
In my society as a child,

00:14:37.530 --> 00:14:40.020
a lot of guys would go to these brothels.

00:14:40.020 --> 00:14:43.755
They still do, this consistent of the morays of that society.

00:14:43.755 --> 00:14:46.980
You weren't rejected for doing that on a,

00:14:46.980 --> 00:14:48.960
on a social basis.

00:14:48.960 --> 00:14:52.095
But is it ethical? Would you do it?

00:14:52.095 --> 00:14:54.850
That's the important question.

00:14:56.480 --> 00:15:01.905
Turn to another couple of business scenarios that I've personally been involved in,

00:15:01.905 --> 00:15:04.380
one was a natural gas research organization.

