Certainly you can come in here and help us convince the regulator to

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:22.005
tax ratepayers at an even higher level so we can do even more R&amp;D.

00:15:22.005 --> 00:15:23.910
And you can tell everybody how,

00:15:23.910 --> 00:15:25.575
how, how beneficial that is.

00:15:25.575 --> 00:15:26.880
Would you do that for us please?

00:15:26.880 --> 00:15:29.290
Would you come in and get us more money?"

00:15:30.170 --> 00:15:33.190
Would you have done their job?

00:15:33.680 --> 00:15:39.105
We didn't do that job. Here's one that we did.

00:15:39.105 --> 00:15:43.110
Climate change, National Energy Technology Laboratory to government body.

00:15:43.110 --> 00:15:44.445
This is in the public domain.

00:15:44.445 --> 00:15:47.430
We need some electricity modelling to show the global warming from

00:15:47.430 --> 00:15:51.735
Ohio coal generation is deleterious to the environment over the long term.

00:15:51.735 --> 00:15:53.610
We've got a lot of money for that,

00:15:53.610 --> 00:15:54.915
we will make it worth your while.

00:15:54.915 --> 00:15:59.430
Would you do it? So, let's talk about Prudential.

00:15:59.430 --> 00:16:00.960
Is it prudential sure?

00:16:00.960 --> 00:16:04.080
You can make some money doing this. Is it legal?

00:16:04.080 --> 00:16:06.660
Sure. Is it ethical?

00:16:06.660 --> 00:16:09.780
I didn't have an ethical problem with it.

00:16:09.780 --> 00:16:12.855
And that kind of simple amounts. Was I lying?

00:16:12.855 --> 00:16:15.165
No. Was I stealing?

00:16:15.165 --> 00:16:16.860
No. Was I harming?

00:16:16.860 --> 00:16:25.020
No. [NOISE] Here's one that's a little more murky and this one occurred some years ago.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:28.425
I had sold company to a very large service firm.

00:16:28.425 --> 00:16:33.090
I became an employee for a spece-specified minimum period of time.

00:16:33.090 --> 00:16:35.460
And when I joined, like most people,

00:16:35.460 --> 00:16:36.600
I was required to sign

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:40.080
an ethics and conflict of interests professional practice agreement.

00:16:40.080 --> 00:16:41.445
I did.

00:16:41.445 --> 00:16:44.130
And there was a meeting of the minds on those agreements.

00:16:44.130 --> 00:16:47.055
In ethics we talk a lot about meeting of the minds.

00:16:47.055 --> 00:16:49.920
What do you expect me to do and what am I going to do?

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.925
I knew what they meant and I I agreed with what they meant.

00:16:53.925 --> 00:16:56.865
I think doing those kinds of things it's not ethical,

00:16:56.865 --> 00:16:58.740
probably not legal as well.

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:03.060
One of the provisions in those agreements as in most that I've seen was,

00:17:03.060 --> 00:17:06.780
that I was not to engage in bribery, gifts, considerations,

00:17:06.780 --> 00:17:09.900
kickbacks, commissions, conflict of interest,

00:17:09.900 --> 00:17:12.480
and certainly no blackmail or extortion.

00:17:12.480 --> 00:17:16.800
And as you think about what we teach in,

00:17:16.800 --> 00:17:19.470
in the ethical analyst and you think about these agreements,

00:17:19.470 --> 00:17:21.750
you much better prepare to understand

00:17:21.750 --> 00:17:24.720
these agreements of what they mean legally and ethically.

00:17:24.720 --> 00:17:29.430
It wasn't long after I joined that I was summoned to a conference call.

00:17:29.430 --> 00:17:32.535
And quite a few partners on that call,

00:17:32.535 --> 00:17:37.020
the company had been initiating very large proposal effort to accompany.

00:17:37.020 --> 00:17:41.310
The person responsible at that company was somebody that I knew.

00:17:41.310 --> 00:17:43.200
And I had worked with her,

00:17:43.200 --> 00:17:47.475
she was a VP in that company in a previous job in a previous company.

00:17:47.475 --> 00:17:49.530
The conference call it something like this,

00:17:49.530 --> 00:17:51.450
Nesbitt, you know their VP Betsy?

00:17:51.450 --> 00:17:53.940
That's not a real name. I Do.

00:17:53.940 --> 00:17:58.320
I worked for as a consultant a couple of years back. Did she like you?

00:17:58.320 --> 00:18:01.530
I never really asked, but I inferred so.

00:18:01.530 --> 00:18:02.760
Let's cut to the chase.

00:18:02.760 --> 00:18:05.055
We want this job and it's competitive.

00:18:05.055 --> 00:18:08.430
We want you Dale to get Betsy and her husband up to lower

00:18:08.430 --> 00:18:11.790
canterra not really the name of the place that we went, to.

00:18:11.790 --> 00:18:14.250
Its a PGA golf course and a resort,

00:18:14.250 --> 00:18:20.440
for a fully paid luxurious relaxing weekend away from the kids fully paid by us.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:25.720
By the way, is she happy with her husband? I was asked.

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:31.620
We need to invite a rep for a brief business meeting on Friday and

00:18:31.620 --> 00:18:34.920
then we'll give her a luxurious weekend with her husband.

00:18:34.920 --> 00:18:38.925
Because we want her to be positively disposed towards our proposal.

00:18:38.925 --> 00:18:42.990
What we'd like you to do Dale,

00:18:42.990 --> 00:18:44.355
is we'd like you to approach her.

00:18:44.355 --> 00:18:46.440
You know her? None of us knows her.

00:18:46.440 --> 00:18:48.120
And we'd like you to propose this.

00:18:48.120 --> 00:18:53.650
[NOISE] Would you have done this?

00:18:55.070 --> 00:18:58.485
Would your ethical code have allowed you to do this?

00:18:58.485 --> 00:19:04.155
Will that legal agreement that you signed with this company have allowed you to do this?

00:19:04.155 --> 00:19:09.045
Well, I said I can't do it and I agreed not to do this kind of thing.

00:19:09.045 --> 00:19:11.880
And there were a couple of people that were pretty

00:19:11.880 --> 00:19:14.565
furious telling me I was letting the company down.

00:19:14.565 --> 00:19:17.805
We need this money to pay the salaries of our people,

00:19:17.805 --> 00:19:19.275
it's a big contract.

00:19:19.275 --> 00:19:20.610
There's no I in team.

00:19:20.610 --> 00:19:21.870
You've gotta be a team player,

00:19:21.870 --> 00:19:24.060
you've got to do this.

00:19:24.060 --> 00:19:26.475
And I replied uh,

00:19:26.475 --> 00:19:28.935
as ethical analyst and said,

00:19:28.935 --> 00:19:33.030
suppose they'd asked me to rob a liquor store, would I do that?

00:19:33.030 --> 00:19:34.200
Would you expect me to do this?

00:19:34.200 --> 00:19:36.600
They said of course not. And I said,

00:19:36.600 --> 00:19:39.450
so you are asking me to break a contract that I

00:19:39.450 --> 00:19:43.275
have with you and to violate my ethical code, the answer is no.

00:19:43.275 --> 00:19:46.350
Ethics is fairly easy when we think about it

00:19:46.350 --> 00:19:48.765
that way is would it have been prudential for me to do it?

00:19:48.765 --> 00:19:52.920
I don't know. Would it have been legal for me to do it?

00:19:52.920 --> 00:19:55.725
Not clear, not clear.

00:19:55.725 --> 00:19:59.190
Maybe not. Would it have been ethical for me to do it?

00:19:59.190 --> 00:20:06.360
No. [NOISE] Isn't a sales commission the same thing?

00:20:06.360 --> 00:20:08.385
Those of you who work under Commission,

00:20:08.385 --> 00:20:11.310
you'll do a lot of things to get a sale.

00:20:11.310 --> 00:20:13.755
Will you do anything unethical?

00:20:13.755 --> 00:20:16.335
We'll talk about that in a minute.

00:20:16.335 --> 00:20:18.630
And [NOISE] the minute is right now.

00:20:18.630 --> 00:20:26.370
Not long thereafter, we submitted a proposal to the North Dakota Highway Department,

00:20:26.370 --> 00:20:28.500
and we won't talk about what it is.

00:20:28.500 --> 00:20:32.680
But it had to do with managing their infrastructure and so forth.

00:20:32.680 --> 00:20:36.770
We worked and worked and worked and submitted what I considered to be

00:20:36.770 --> 00:20:41.575
an honest and attractive proposal at a budget of $1.5 million.

00:20:41.575 --> 00:20:45.270
The amount is fictitious but the uh, point is made.

00:20:45.270 --> 00:20:46.680
On the day of the award,

00:20:46.680 --> 00:20:49.170
I phoned them up and I asked um,

00:20:49.170 --> 00:20:51.450
"How did you like our proposal?" They said, "We loved it.

00:20:51.450 --> 00:20:53.490
It was the best proposal by four.

00:20:53.490 --> 00:20:55.425
We want to do business with you guys.

00:20:55.425 --> 00:20:58.815
It's hidden shoulders above the other proposals that we got.

00:20:58.815 --> 00:21:00.195
There were five others.

00:21:00.195 --> 00:21:04.485
However, you're five times the budget of that.

00:21:04.485 --> 00:21:08.100
Aren't you smart enough to know that what you do is you

00:21:08.100 --> 00:21:11.985
bid way below your cost if you ever want to get in the door here?

00:21:11.985 --> 00:21:14.430
And then you look for um,

00:21:14.430 --> 00:21:16.955
uh, ways to augment the budget.

00:21:16.955 --> 00:21:19.205
You can't tell the truth in these bids.

00:21:19.205 --> 00:21:22.280
What you have to do is reduce your cost

00:21:22.280 --> 00:21:26.320
to the point where you are cost competitive and then look for change orders.

00:21:26.320 --> 00:21:29.340
So, give you one chance here.

00:21:29.340 --> 00:21:31.440
And if you can do it, I'm not even sure.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:34.065
If you can reduce your bid by 80 percent,

00:21:34.065 --> 00:21:35.535
will, we will reconsider you.

00:21:35.535 --> 00:21:37.810
You'll be out of the competitive range."

00:21:37.810 --> 00:21:44.940
Would you do that? Would you write a proposal to do something that you know you can't do?

00:21:45.310 --> 00:21:48.245
I didn't. I said, "I can't do that.

00:21:48.245 --> 00:21:52.240
It's just not okay to do that." Is it legal?

00:21:52.240 --> 00:21:54.180
Yeah. Is it prudential?

00:21:54.180 --> 00:21:55.395
Yeah. Is it ethical?

00:21:55.395 --> 00:21:58.140
Is it right? No, it's not.

00:21:58.140 --> 00:22:01.260
[NOISE] I'm representing in writing that

00:22:01.260 --> 00:22:04.740
I can do something that I know I can't do for the,

00:22:04.740 --> 00:22:06.705
for the budget that they have.

00:22:06.705 --> 00:22:10.410
There's a really interesting structure out there,

00:22:10.410 --> 00:22:13.545
called the Eastern span of the San Francisco Bay Bridge.

00:22:13.545 --> 00:22:16.410
It was built on exactly that kind of basis,

00:22:16.410 --> 00:22:19.080
the original contract will get a billion dollars on it.

00:22:19.080 --> 00:22:22.035
It came in at over six billion dollars.

00:22:22.035 --> 00:22:24.075
And it was all change orders.

00:22:24.075 --> 00:22:26.595
I would argue and in the public press,

00:22:26.595 --> 00:22:30.105
people knew they could never do it for the bid that they offered.

00:22:30.105 --> 00:22:33.870
But they offered those bids and look for the change orders.

00:22:33.870 --> 00:22:37.780
Is that ethical? Is that's something that you would do?

00:22:40.890 --> 00:22:44.860
Another personal vignette from my own practice,

00:22:44.860 --> 00:22:47.695
uh, was a International Resource Company.

00:22:47.695 --> 00:22:49.675
It wasn't an oil company, uh,

00:22:49.675 --> 00:22:52.720
but it was a company like an oil company.

00:22:52.720 --> 00:22:59.515
I'll call them, uh, as a pseudonym River Bend Company. This is very insightful.

00:22:59.515 --> 00:23:03.850
They're one of the larger international resources, uh, producers in the world.

00:23:03.850 --> 00:23:05.500
They're headquartered out of the US.

00:23:05.500 --> 00:23:09.760
We had a fixed price contract with this organization,

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:11.560
true amounts are disguised.

00:23:11.560 --> 00:23:13.180
Everything was agreed to.

00:23:13.180 --> 00:23:14.470
We had a meeting of the minds.

00:23:14.470 --> 00:23:15.550
We knew what we were gonna do,

00:23:15.550 --> 00:23:17.170
they knew what we were gonna do.

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:19.225
Now, the client who was, uh,

00:23:19.225 --> 00:23:20.705
not really named Bob,

00:23:20.705 --> 00:23:22.575
but we'll use that pseudonym.

00:23:22.575 --> 00:23:24.810
All along the way kept asking for more.

00:23:24.810 --> 00:23:27.285
"Hey, the boss wants to understand CO2 better,

00:23:27.285 --> 00:23:28.605
let's get it in there and understand.

00:23:28.605 --> 00:23:30.270
Let's run 10 more scenarios.

00:23:30.270 --> 00:23:32.040
Let's get the client point of view,

00:23:32.040 --> 00:23:35.585
and then the boss really wants to understand CO2 remediation."

00:23:35.585 --> 00:23:37.765
All good objectives, right?

00:23:37.765 --> 00:23:41.410
And so we said, "That's good.

00:23:41.410 --> 00:23:43.660
We're glad to expand the scope to take care of it."

00:23:43.660 --> 00:23:44.950
And Bob kept assuring us.

00:23:44.950 --> 00:23:46.420
"Don't worry, you'll get paid.

00:23:46.420 --> 00:23:48.250
No sweat, just get going, just get going.

00:23:48.250 --> 00:23:50.605
I'll take care of the contract and the payment.

00:23:50.605 --> 00:23:52.000
I'll have it to you next week.

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.980
We cannot wait. Just go."

00:23:53.980 --> 00:23:58.045
We must have had that conversation five or more times,

00:23:58.045 --> 00:24:02.245
with me getting nervous that we didn't have a contract for this performance.

00:24:02.245 --> 00:24:05.080
That was unequivocal insurance- assurance.

00:24:05.080 --> 00:24:07.210
We came to the end, the client called and said

00:24:07.210 --> 00:24:10.030
the proverbial good news, bad news preamble.

00:24:10.030 --> 00:24:13.540
"Fantastic job, our CEO JB loved it.

00:24:13.540 --> 00:24:18.640
However, I'm sorry I didn't budget or enable the initial work all along the way,

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:20.900
even though I told you I would.

00:24:21.210 --> 00:24:24.835
You can't do it now, there are realities.

00:24:24.835 --> 00:24:26.860
My management won't accept it.

00:24:26.860 --> 00:24:29.840
They aren't expecting. I can't do it.

00:24:30.180 --> 00:24:34.150
You're gonna have to accept the original budget as payment in full.

00:24:34.150 --> 00:24:35.410
You're gonna have to forego

00:24:35.410 --> 00:24:40.870
the 50 percent additional budget that I told you that I would get you and I didn't.

00:24:40.870 --> 00:24:44.005
But Dale we were happy.

00:24:44.005 --> 00:24:46.690
I know that I verbally authorized this,

00:24:46.690 --> 00:24:48.340
and I know you did it and delivered it.

00:24:48.340 --> 00:24:51.370
I know you really should get that additional money, because we agreed.

00:24:51.370 --> 00:24:53.020
Alas, I just can't come through.

00:24:53.020 --> 00:24:55.790
This is final, take it or leave it."

00:24:56.100 --> 00:24:59.020
What I said was, "This is unacceptable,

00:24:59.020 --> 00:25:00.475
and you gave me your word.

00:25:00.475 --> 00:25:02.440
You told me that you were gonna pay me."

00:25:02.440 --> 00:25:04.795
" I know I gave you my word,

00:25:04.795 --> 00:25:07.000
but I didn't, I can't, I won't.

00:25:07.000 --> 00:25:09.680
You deserve it. I'm not gonna do it."

00:25:09.690 --> 00:25:11.740
Then it got really tough.

00:25:11.740 --> 00:25:14.800
He said, "If you send me an invoice for the original amount, I'll get it paid.

00:25:14.800 --> 00:25:16.330
If you invoice me for a dime more,

00:25:16.330 --> 00:25:17.980
I'll say there's no contract,

00:25:17.980 --> 00:25:20.560
and they'll drag out over a major hassle.

00:25:20.560 --> 00:25:22.570
It might be a months or two months,

00:25:22.570 --> 00:25:25.850
maybe a year before you ever get paid anything."

00:25:26.580 --> 00:25:29.275
I said, "You lied to me."

00:25:29.275 --> 00:25:31.330
He said, "Take it or leave it. That's final."

00:25:31.330 --> 00:25:32.380
I said, "I'll accept it,

00:25:32.380 --> 00:25:33.460
I don't have a choice.

00:25:33.460 --> 00:25:35.815
I've already paid my people and I need to cover."

00:25:35.815 --> 00:25:38.605
And you go,"Sorry, I let you down.

00:25:38.605 --> 00:25:41.230
I didn't get the paperwork done cause management was

00:25:41.230 --> 00:25:43.660
complaining about the cost of this project.

00:25:43.660 --> 00:25:46.570
And I wasn't gonna raise my hand and ask for more money.

00:25:46.570 --> 00:25:48.309
I'm sure you understand."

00:25:48.309 --> 00:25:50.605
"No, Bob, I don't."

00:25:50.605 --> 00:25:52.705
So, here's the funny part.

00:25:52.705 --> 00:25:55.705
I send an invoice for a hundred and fifty K as I agreed.

00:25:55.705 --> 00:25:58.840
I get a check two weeks later for a hundred and fifty K on Monday.

00:25:58.840 --> 00:26:03.100
On Wednesday, I get a second check for a hundred and fifty K by accident.

00:26:03.100 --> 00:26:06.205
They had made an accounting error and they paid me twice.

00:26:06.205 --> 00:26:10.405
So, I had in my hands more than we had agreed to,

00:26:10.405 --> 00:26:14.600
including the authorized budget in the middle.

00:26:15.750 --> 00:26:18.445
I had his life in my hands tonight.

00:26:18.445 --> 00:26:21.200
In my shoes, what would you have done?

00:26:21.930 --> 00:26:25.885
What would you have done? Providentially, what would you have done?

00:26:25.885 --> 00:26:31.225
You kept the money, legally, probably okay.

00:26:31.225 --> 00:26:34.010
Ethically, what would you have done?

00:26:34.050 --> 00:26:37.750
What I did was, I called him up and said,

00:26:37.750 --> 00:26:40.360
"Hey man, thanks for the seventy five thousand dollar tip.

00:26:40.360 --> 00:26:43.990
That was great." "What?" You said you paid me twice.

00:26:43.990 --> 00:26:46.510
I didn't know you like the work so much.

00:26:46.510 --> 00:26:49.675
Out comes the ethical confession of sins.

00:26:49.675 --> 00:26:51.250
"Oh my God. There goes my job.

00:26:51.250 --> 00:26:53.215
I'm gonna get fired.

00:26:53.215 --> 00:26:56.770
You're gonna keep the money and blow my budget. I know you'll do that.

00:26:56.770 --> 00:26:59.140
I might be forced out of the company over this.

00:26:59.140 --> 00:27:01.495
I never told management a word about it.

00:27:01.495 --> 00:27:03.130
I expanded scope on you,

00:27:03.130 --> 00:27:04.450
knowing that we wouldn't pay."

00:27:04.450 --> 00:27:05.800
Et cetera, et cetera, et cetera.

00:27:05.800 --> 00:27:07.300
He confessed sin after sin,

00:27:07.300 --> 00:27:08.890
lie, after lie, after lie.

00:27:08.890 --> 00:27:15.190
And one of the things we talk about a lot in the ethi- ethical analyst is,

00:27:15.190 --> 00:27:16.840
if you grab one paper clip,

00:27:16.840 --> 00:27:19.345
the whole stack comes, just like lies.

00:27:19.345 --> 00:27:20.560
You tell one lie,

00:27:20.560 --> 00:27:22.630
the whole set of lies follows,

00:27:22.630 --> 00:27:27.530
doesn't it? What did I do?

00:27:27.600 --> 00:27:31.360
And I have a lot of arguments with my colleagues.

00:27:31.360 --> 00:27:33.520
The key was my words,"I'll accept it."

00:27:33.520 --> 00:27:35.080
I didn't say,"I might accept it.

00:27:35.080 --> 00:27:36.175
",I said," I'll accept it."

00:27:36.175 --> 00:27:38.200
I have no choice. I've already paid my people.

00:27:38.200 --> 00:27:41.035
I need to cover as I won't be paid.

00:27:41.035 --> 00:27:43.660
So I said, "I'm not dishonest.

00:27:43.660 --> 00:27:47.260
I told you I would accept a hundred and fifty K, even under duress.

00:27:47.260 --> 00:27:51.265
I dropped that second check uncashed in the mail back to you this morning.

00:27:51.265 --> 00:27:56.725
I won't be paid for doing your project and you're dishonesty is responsible for that."

00:27:56.725 --> 00:27:59.365
He said, "I can't believe you did that."

00:27:59.365 --> 00:28:01.015
But there's a key comment.

00:28:01.015 --> 00:28:02.500
I said, "Yeah, I know Bob,

00:28:02.500 --> 00:28:03.550
you would have kept the money.

00:28:03.550 --> 00:28:05.350
I know you would have. This is the first time

00:28:05.350 --> 00:28:08.330
you've actually told me the truth on the project."

00:28:08.940 --> 00:28:12.265
It brings up another point we talk about in ethics,

00:28:12.265 --> 00:28:13.300
is your word you bond?

00:28:13.300 --> 00:28:15.805
And my ethical code which I don't share,

00:28:15.805 --> 00:28:18.010
my word is my bond.

00:28:18.010 --> 00:28:22.060
And in student ethical codes that we write in class,

00:28:22.060 --> 00:28:24.970
they're very personal, they're well kept in strict confidence.

00:28:24.970 --> 00:28:26.830
Nobody sees anybody ethical, ah,

00:28:26.830 --> 00:28:30.175
sees anybody ethical code, and you'll see why.

00:28:30.175 --> 00:28:33.535
Okay. Did I do the right thing?

00:28:33.535 --> 00:28:35.305
Here's a famous one,

00:28:35.305 --> 00:28:38.950
and this is Harry Truman at the end of World War Two.

00:28:38.950 --> 00:28:42.100
We talk a lot about consequentialism and ethics,

00:28:42.100 --> 00:28:44.770
but here was one where, um,

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:46.960
there had been, um,

00:28:46.960 --> 00:28:49.720
a very bloody war that had been going on for four years.

00:28:49.720 --> 00:28:55.600
Estimates of deaths in World War Two were 77.5 million people around the world died.

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:59.440
Harry Truman said, "It'll be 500,000 to a

00:28:59.440 --> 00:29:03.550
million people will die if we have to invade the Japanese mainland.

00:29:03.550 --> 00:29:06.175
If we dropped the atomic bombs,

00:29:06.175 --> 00:29:08.500
those people won't die."

00:29:08.500 --> 00:29:14.260
Okay. People in Japan are dying anyway under the firebombings that we're doing.

00:29:14.260 --> 00:29:16.045
And so, he said,

00:29:16.045 --> 00:29:19.525
"Let's do a benefit cost analysis of this."

00:29:19.525 --> 00:29:22.510
And the benefit cost analysis was very simple.

00:29:22.510 --> 00:29:25.640
We save half-a-million to a million lives.

00:29:26.610 --> 00:29:30.010
And we save the fire bombings on their side.

00:29:30.010 --> 00:29:33.265
So, dropping the atomic bombs that the, er,

00:29:33.265 --> 00:29:36.730
United States ended up dropping was actually a net positive,

00:29:36.730 --> 00:29:40.810
and he- uh, that's a consequentialist ethical argument.

00:29:40.810 --> 00:29:45.669
And consequences means, you do a benefit cost analysis of lying,

00:29:45.669 --> 00:29:47.425
or harming, or anything else.

00:29:47.425 --> 00:29:49.795
And if the benefits exceed the cost,

00:29:49.795 --> 00:29:51.820
it's ethically respectable to do it.

00:29:51.820 --> 00:29:53.770
That was the analysis that was made.

00:29:53.770 --> 00:29:56.050
I would suggest if you're interested in this,

00:29:56.050 --> 00:29:59.290
read Richard Rhodes 'The Making of the Atomic Bomb'.

00:29:59.290 --> 00:30:02.815
We teach a lot about consequentialist ethics,

00:30:02.815 --> 00:30:05.080
and we teach a lot about action-based ethics.

00:30:05.080 --> 00:30:09.565
And that was a classic example that we try to analyze and think through.

00:30:09.565 --> 00:30:12.190
One of the other things that we know is that,

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:15.625
if we accept public office or run for public office,

00:30:15.625 --> 00:30:17.800
we will be consequentialist in our,

00:30:17.800 --> 00:30:19.315
uh, in our decision-making.

00:30:19.315 --> 00:30:22.555
We will have to assess aggregate benefits and costs.

00:30:22.555 --> 00:30:25.015
And for things like dropping an atomic bomb,

00:30:25.015 --> 00:30:26.875
maybe we don't want to do that.

00:30:26.875 --> 00:30:28.315
Maybe we wanna say,

00:30:28.315 --> 00:30:32.440
"We don't drop it under any circumstance no matter what the consequences."

00:30:32.440 --> 00:30:34.705
Okay. We're going to pass that.

00:30:34.705 --> 00:30:38.410
Here's another ethical issue we're seeing all the time in the United States.

00:30:38.410 --> 00:30:42.805
Would you own a handgun? Is it ethical to own one?

00:30:42.805 --> 00:30:45.715
Is it ethical to carry?

00:30:45.715 --> 00:30:47.890
Would you apply for a permit?

00:30:47.890 --> 00:30:50.260
And if you've got it, would you carry it?

00:30:50.260 --> 00:30:54.565
This brings up a point in ethics that imposing harm

00:30:54.565 --> 00:30:59.200
or on a person's- person or property is clearly unethical.

00:30:59.200 --> 00:31:01.525
But what about imposing risk?

00:31:01.525 --> 00:31:04.750
This imposes a lot of risk on people.

00:31:04.750 --> 00:31:07.840
And, er, imposing a risk has

00:31:07.840 --> 00:31:12.115
ethical consequences too, doesn't it, serious ethical consequences.

00:31:12.115 --> 00:31:17.260
We think that imposing risk on people is unethical.

00:31:17.260 --> 00:31:22.600
Would you own a male pit bull unspayed and put the male pit bull in your backyard?

00:31:22.600 --> 00:31:24.520
That's imposing risk on people.

00:31:24.520 --> 00:31:25.735
Many people think too.

00:31:25.735 --> 00:31:31.090
Would you do it? And notice in the commentary we have here,

00:31:31.090 --> 00:31:33.115
I keep saying, would you do it?

00:31:33.115 --> 00:31:35.185
Would you make that decision?

00:31:35.185 --> 00:31:37.150
And that's the centerpiece of ethics, isn't it?

00:31:37.150 --> 00:31:38.950
It's what do you do,

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:41.500
or what you not do.

00:31:41.500 --> 00:31:44.860
The thing we focus on a lot, and,

00:31:44.860 --> 00:31:47.920
um, we see it every for a white lies.

00:31:47.920 --> 00:31:52.240
I find it difficult to dis- to discern lies by color.

00:31:52.240 --> 00:31:55.480
But white lies are the harmless lives that are told,

00:31:55.480 --> 00:31:58.690
uh, to save the feelings of another person.

00:31:58.690 --> 00:32:00.910
The classical white lie is,"Honey,

00:32:00.910 --> 00:32:03.500
do I look fat in these pants?"

00:32:03.500 --> 00:32:06.175
What are you supposed to say,

00:32:06.175 --> 00:32:08.290
from an ethical perspective?

00:32:08.290 --> 00:32:09.730
Are you supposed to say,"No.

00:32:09.730 --> 00:32:12.099
You- you look really slim, you look ravishing."

00:32:12.099 --> 00:32:14.290
Are you supposed to say,"You know,

00:32:14.290 --> 00:32:16.855
I love you anyway, no matter what your weight is."

00:32:16.855 --> 00:32:20.740
And, one of the things that we note is that,

00:32:20.740 --> 00:32:22.870
there is ethical content in your answer.

00:32:22.870 --> 00:32:25.150
If you're willing to lie about the small things,

00:32:25.150 --> 00:32:27.760
are you willing to lie about the large things?

00:32:27.760 --> 00:32:33.380
Very interesting white lies may not be as innocuous as you'd think.

00:32:33.480 --> 00:32:36.640
Romantic involvement in the workplace is

00:32:36.640 --> 00:32:40.210
another issue that we- we see time and time again as not.

00:32:40.210 --> 00:32:41.830
[NOISE] We see it a lot.

00:32:41.830 --> 00:32:44.095
And, uh, and, uh,

00:32:44.095 --> 00:32:46.450
back in the, uh, in the,

00:32:46.450 --> 00:32:51.100
um, um, uh, old days,

00:32:51.100 --> 00:32:53.890
maybe ten years ago there were no corporate rules against it.

00:32:53.890 --> 00:32:55.840
Now there are. Does that matter?

00:32:55.840 --> 00:33:01.030
Does it matter whether there are rules against it or not in your ethical code?

00:33:01.030 --> 00:33:04.330
Ask, the rules are kind of a legal thing, right?

00:33:04.330 --> 00:33:07.150
It may be illegal to have this certainly against

00:33:07.150 --> 00:33:10.075
the rules or you've signed a contract not to do it.

00:33:10.075 --> 00:33:14.870
But suppose you didn't have them? Is it okay?

00:33:16.500 --> 00:33:19.720
I'll give you one other example from my practice.

00:33:19.720 --> 00:33:22.150
This has happened in everybody's practice.

00:33:22.150 --> 00:33:24.565
Um, I was, um,

00:33:24.565 --> 00:33:27.220
working with the client organization with a team

00:33:27.220 --> 00:33:30.250
of about four of them and about four of us.

00:33:30.250 --> 00:33:34.120
And one of the client's persons was a lady named Ann,

00:33:34.120 --> 00:33:37.180
junior professional in their organization,

00:33:37.180 --> 00:33:41.425
single, attractive, very forward in her behavior.

00:33:41.425 --> 00:33:47.770
She became very flirtatious with one of our senior guys, okay?

00:33:47.770 --> 00:33:51.055
And uh, particularly at this-

00:33:51.055 --> 00:33:55.795
the dinner and drinks events that you invariably have after work.

00:33:55.795 --> 00:34:00.850
Um, they uh, they left arm in arm from one of

00:34:00.850 --> 00:34:06.325
those events and didn't resurface till the next morning.

00:34:06.325 --> 00:34:13.610
Now, Garry, our guy was married with kids. She wasn't.

00:34:15.210 --> 00:34:19.780
Was her behavior ethical? Was legal.

00:34:19.780 --> 00:34:22.300
Was it prudential? I mean,

00:34:22.300 --> 00:34:24.940
she wouldn't know, right? Was it ethical?

00:34:24.940 --> 00:34:27.370
Was his behavior ethical?

00:34:27.370 --> 00:34:30.715
Here's the other one, was my behavior ethical?

00:34:30.715 --> 00:34:32.780
I didn't tell anybody about it.

00:34:32.780 --> 00:34:36.495
Now, I know that in modern companies you have er,

00:34:36.495 --> 00:34:39.885
you gotta tell about these kind of things inside your company.

00:34:39.885 --> 00:34:43.080
Would you tell your senior management that uh,

00:34:43.080 --> 00:34:47.230
that that Garry had breached his, his contract?

00:34:48.330 --> 00:34:52.310
Interesting ethical questions, aren't they?

00:34:53.610 --> 00:34:58.240
It's one of my favorites because I have been so tempted so many times.

00:34:58.240 --> 00:35:02.725
How many times have you been at a hotel and you walk by a table and that's what you see?

00:35:02.725 --> 00:35:09.325
And you look over there and this is a conference for hairdressing and makeup.

00:35:09.325 --> 00:35:10.780
So, it's not your conference,

00:35:10.780 --> 00:35:13.225
you have no idea who these people are.

00:35:13.225 --> 00:35:15.490
Is it okay to say you know,

00:35:15.490 --> 00:35:17.710
they're not going to miss accomplishment and a cup,

00:35:17.710 --> 00:35:19.120
a cup of coffee and uh,

00:35:19.120 --> 00:35:21.055
uh, some nice condiments, are they?

00:35:21.055 --> 00:35:23.440
I'll just go over and help myself.

00:35:23.440 --> 00:35:27.115
The marginal cost is zero to them, right?

00:35:27.115 --> 00:35:31.520
That's the rationale. Is it okay to do that?

00:35:31.590 --> 00:35:36.865
More generally, if somebody else doesn't need something,

00:35:36.865 --> 00:35:39.940
is it okay for you to pick it?

00:35:39.940 --> 00:35:43.435
Adds a really penetrating ethical question.

00:35:43.435 --> 00:35:46.075
It's a question of our day, isn't it?

00:35:46.075 --> 00:35:48.715
So do you belly up and take it?

00:35:48.715 --> 00:35:50.650
I don't. I don't. I couldn't.

00:35:50.650 --> 00:35:52.360
I wouldn't be able to sleep nights.

00:35:52.360 --> 00:35:54.040
It's not my property.

00:35:54.040 --> 00:35:55.720
I don't do things like that,

00:35:55.720 --> 00:35:58.525
but I see people bellying up all the time.

00:35:58.525 --> 00:36:02.665
Okay. Let's skip by this one.

00:36:02.665 --> 00:36:06.220
This would be how we're doing on time pretty close to the end.

00:36:06.220 --> 00:36:08.440
Okay, this will be the last one.

00:36:08.440 --> 00:36:12.655
Many of you have read about Elizabeth Holmes and Theranos.

00:36:12.655 --> 00:36:16.975
And she was the one who- it's Theranos who's the one who said,

00:36:16.975 --> 00:36:19.630
"We only need a tiny drop of blood,

00:36:19.630 --> 00:36:23.500
and those of you who have needle phobia just give a little finger prick.

00:36:23.500 --> 00:36:27.760
And we can do everything with that tiny drop of blood that we could have done with

00:36:27.760 --> 00:36:30.100
the large vial of blood that we normally

00:36:30.100 --> 00:36:34.135
withdraw from your arm and sent through a standard laboratory."

00:36:34.135 --> 00:36:40.105
And there was a number of allegations of fraudulent behavior and so forth like that uh,

00:36:40.105 --> 00:36:42.040
which we'll get into in just one minute.

00:36:42.040 --> 00:36:47.530
Uh, ultimately uh, she and her CEO were

00:36:47.530 --> 00:36:50.560
alleged to have committed fraud with regard to

00:36:50.560 --> 00:36:54.130
the directors and to the investors in their company.

00:36:54.130 --> 00:36:57.595
Because what they had been doing was taking, uh,

00:36:57.595 --> 00:36:59.995
a standard sized vial of blood from people

00:36:59.995 --> 00:37:03.265
rather than putting it through their own technology that didn't work,

00:37:03.265 --> 00:37:08.305
they allegedly send it out to standard laboratories and then reported it back.

00:37:08.305 --> 00:37:12.295
And this gets into, into, into,

00:37:12.295 --> 00:37:16.675
uh, a word that, that we hear all the time it's called spin.

00:37:16.675 --> 00:37:19.100
What does spin mean?

00:37:19.290 --> 00:37:23.650
Means lie. And one of the things we talk

00:37:23.650 --> 00:37:27.520
a lot about in the ethical analyst in our analysis of ethics is,

00:37:27.520 --> 00:37:30.760
let's don't let euphemisms think for us.

00:37:30.760 --> 00:37:34.780
Euphemisms like lie, like racial epithets, things like that.

00:37:34.780 --> 00:37:36.550
These were all lies.

00:37:36.550 --> 00:37:40.000
You can't be meaning that because the meaning is so broad.

00:37:40.000 --> 00:37:42.320
Spin. What is spin?

00:37:43.620 --> 00:37:47.725
Reading from a paper or article on January 29th,

00:37:47.725 --> 00:37:49.330
"Footage from company parties and

00:37:49.330 --> 00:37:52.540
internal interviews made the whole thing come alive and helps paint

00:37:52.540 --> 00:37:58.390
a full picture of what was happening inside this now infamously secretive shop."

00:37:58.390 --> 00:38:01.435
This is the, the sentence that really got me.

00:38:01.435 --> 00:38:06.040
I think one of Elizabeth's greatest skills at least in terms of Theranos initiative

00:38:06.040 --> 00:38:11.680
is her ability as a storyteller, a storyteller.

00:38:11.680 --> 00:38:14.095
What's a storytelling?

00:38:14.095 --> 00:38:16.525
It's a liar, right?

00:38:16.525 --> 00:38:21.265
It's a story. This, this is a euphemism, okay?

00:38:21.265 --> 00:38:25.105
She was able to be so convincing because she convinced herself.

00:38:25.105 --> 00:38:28.300
She convinced herself that the righteousness of her cause

00:38:28.300 --> 00:38:31.435
was so great that if they cut a few corners here and there,

00:38:31.435 --> 00:38:35.395
the'll get a few bad test that they get it right ultimately and save lives.

00:38:35.395 --> 00:38:42.940
That idea that the ends justify the means isn't particularly well time-tested, is it?

00:38:42.940 --> 00:38:45.310
The interesting question is,

00:38:45.310 --> 00:38:47.360
would you hire her?

00:38:48.450 --> 00:38:52.210
Would you hire her? Was it prudential for her to do this?

00:38:52.210 --> 00:38:54.115
It turns out it wasn't.

00:38:54.115 --> 00:38:56.935
Was it legal? Turns out it wasn't.

00:38:56.935 --> 00:38:58.405
But was it ethical?

00:38:58.405 --> 00:38:59.770
Is it ethical to say, "Well,

00:38:59.770 --> 00:39:01.405
we'll get it right next time."

00:39:01.405 --> 00:39:04.570
We'll represent the final, uh,

00:39:04.570 --> 00:39:11.450
objective that we're, we're looking for rather than the realities along the way.

00:39:14.460 --> 00:39:16.840
So, when we think about ethics,

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:21.625
we really want to think in summary of- flip to the summary then we can talk.

00:39:21.625 --> 00:39:24.715
This was another one. Let's talk very briefly.

00:39:24.715 --> 00:39:29.290
My company developed the original seat pricing algorithm for United Airlines and others.

00:39:29.290 --> 00:39:33.925
It's well-known. It's a very interesting ethical question that comes out of that.

00:39:33.925 --> 00:39:36.775
What we helped that airline do was charge

00:39:36.775 --> 00:39:41.230
different prices to different people for the exact same thing.

00:39:41.230 --> 00:39:44.215
A seat on an airplane from LA to New York.

00:39:44.215 --> 00:39:47.635
Is that ethical? Is it ethical to charge different people

00:39:47.635 --> 00:39:54.170
dramatically different prices for the exact same thing? It happens all the time.

00:39:54.270 --> 00:39:57.460
Okay, let's conclude then with uh,

00:39:57.460 --> 00:40:02.185
some of the final thoughts that we have and then we'll open up the questions.

00:40:02.185 --> 00:40:04.840
Ethical codes and ethics,

00:40:04.840 --> 00:40:07.180
the way I see it, have to be actionable.

00:40:07.180 --> 00:40:09.460
You actually have to be able to do something or

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:12.415
not do something based on your ethical code.

00:40:12.415 --> 00:40:14.950
So, abstract, theoretical, hypothetical,

00:40:14.950 --> 00:40:18.820
non-actionable conse- consequences don't really cut the mustard.

00:40:18.820 --> 00:40:22.045
My ethical code doesn't have cure world hunger in.

00:40:22.045 --> 00:40:23.470
How can we ever do that?

00:40:23.470 --> 00:40:27.085
Would be a nice ethical thing to do but it's not actionable.

00:40:27.085 --> 00:40:30.025
The other thing is, ethics is not coercible.

00:40:30.025 --> 00:40:32.470
You can't force somebody to be ethical.

00:40:32.470 --> 00:40:34.750
Ethic's all this, ethics is relates to

00:40:34.750 --> 00:40:38.005
the decisions that you make when no one's watching you.

00:40:38.005 --> 00:40:40.360
When no one's coercing you.

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:45.505
It's what you do when you turn out the lights and really make that decision.

00:40:45.505 --> 00:40:47.560
And the point of ethics is not to bash

00:40:47.560 --> 00:40:50.560
others on their ethical behavior. That's not what it's about.

00:40:50.560 --> 00:40:54.205
It's about to formulate what's ethical and what's not,

00:40:54.205 --> 00:40:56.350
what's right and wrong, and what's not.

00:40:56.350 --> 00:40:59.005
Which decisions are justifiable.

00:40:59.005 --> 00:41:01.945
One of the other things that we've perceived as

00:41:01.945 --> 00:41:06.415
Decision Analysts is that ethics may well be rational and self-interested.

00:41:06.415 --> 00:41:07.630
A lot of people say well,

00:41:07.630 --> 00:41:10.045
if you're ethical you're like lambs to the slaughter.

00:41:10.045 --> 00:41:14.500
You're exposing your soft underside to unethical behavior by others.

00:41:14.500 --> 00:41:18.670
I'm not so sure that's an empirically valid uh, assessment.

00:41:18.670 --> 00:41:22.570
Quite the contrary, ethical behavior tends to be,

00:41:22.570 --> 00:41:26.630
I think, uh, rewarded by ethic behavior.

00:41:26.640 --> 00:41:33.025
A famous book by Robert Axelrod from the '80s called The Evolution of Cooperation,

00:41:33.025 --> 00:41:40.760
talks about reinforced positive behavior induces counterparties to behave that way.

00:41:41.300 --> 00:41:47.370
The ethical analyst is replete with these kinds of ethical situations.

00:41:47.370 --> 00:41:50.175
We don't talk politics that gets in the way.

00:41:50.175 --> 00:41:51.885
We don't talk labels.

00:41:51.885 --> 00:41:54.600
We don't label. If people talk labels in the class,

00:41:54.600 --> 00:41:56.250
we eliminate the labels.

00:41:56.250 --> 00:42:01.110
It's about- not about promoting a specific brand of ethics,

00:42:01.110 --> 00:42:05.670
consequentialism, action-based ethics, virtue ethics et cetera.

00:42:05.670 --> 00:42:10.050
We do present a broad range of historical thought on ethics.

00:42:10.050 --> 00:42:13.650
But it's about raising ethical sensitivities and providing

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:18.460
tools for making the right decisions, ethical decisions.

00:42:18.460 --> 00:42:21.230
For those of you who won't be taking the course,

00:42:21.230 --> 00:42:25.250
I would strongly recommend you grab the book that was written for this course about

00:42:25.250 --> 00:42:29.760
ten years ago by retired Professor Ronald Howard and Clint Korver,

00:42:29.760 --> 00:42:31.440
Ethics for the Real World.

00:42:31.440 --> 00:42:36.345
It's available on Amazon for about 20 bucks Kindle, 30 bucks hard.

00:42:36.345 --> 00:42:38.220
We use it in the course.

00:42:38.220 --> 00:42:41.160
The last point- and thank you very much for,

00:42:41.160 --> 00:42:43.500
uh, your attendance at the, at the webinar.

00:42:43.500 --> 00:42:47.775
Ethics professors complain all the time ubiquitously.

00:42:47.775 --> 00:42:49.815
No one ever actually does this.

00:42:49.815 --> 00:42:52.905
They love the course but they never do it.

00:42:52.905 --> 00:42:57.660
And what we found is that there's a much higher possibility of

00:42:57.660 --> 00:43:02.940
people behaving eth- ethically when they write an ethical code.

00:43:02.940 --> 00:43:06.540
Why is it that? It's like practicing the piano.

00:43:06.540 --> 00:43:09.285
You figure out what you need to do.

00:43:09.285 --> 00:43:11.775
What are the exercises you need to do,

00:43:11.775 --> 00:43:13.800
you make a commitment to do that.

00:43:13.800 --> 00:43:15.450
That's what an ethical code is.

00:43:15.450 --> 00:43:18.525
It's getting the principles together,

00:43:18.525 --> 00:43:22.800
getting a good understanding of them so when the ethical situations occur,

00:43:22.800 --> 00:43:26.340
the speed of occurrence doesn't get in the way of just saying,

00:43:26.340 --> 00:43:27.540
oh, to heck with ethics.

00:43:27.540 --> 00:43:29.490
I'm just gonna do what's prudential.

00:43:29.490 --> 00:43:31.050
So, ah, you know,

00:43:31.050 --> 00:43:33.810
you mentioned about the tools for ethical decision-making.

00:43:33.810 --> 00:43:38.970
Um, and I know here and elsewhere you've talked about disciplined decision-making,

00:43:38.970 --> 00:43:40.500
uh, if that happens around ethics.

00:43:40.500 --> 00:43:43.275
It sounds like the first step is writing that ethical code.

00:43:43.275 --> 00:43:46.140
Uh, what are some other ways to achieve

00:43:46.140 --> 00:43:49.140
that discipline and what are some of the barriers to acting ethically?

00:43:49.140 --> 00:43:51.735
So, just to, to bring up a few examples.

00:43:51.735 --> 00:43:53.940
Uh, you mentioned, for instance,

